WEBVTT

00:13.206 --> 00:15.167
[SPEAKER_00]: So good evening, everybody.

00:15.187 --> 00:16.608
[SPEAKER_00]: Very happy to be here in New York.

00:45.722 --> 00:49.224
[SPEAKER_00]: something looks like a spaceship.

00:50.984 --> 00:56.166
[SPEAKER_00]: I've never given talk in a spaceship before.

01:02.249 --> 01:12.673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm really happy to be here because many people that I met before when I came with Khyentse Rinpoche

01:14.293 --> 01:19.940
[SPEAKER_00]: in 1975, and then a few times after.

01:20.360 --> 01:21.341
[SPEAKER_00]: So many old friends.

01:22.543 --> 01:26.187
[SPEAKER_00]: So we are all, how do you say, getting a little bit older.

01:28.269 --> 01:39.895
[SPEAKER_00]: And here in the audience, I see maybe like four different generations of Buddhists in America.

01:40.635 --> 01:43.776
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'm a little bit not sure how to talk.

01:45.297 --> 01:52.799
[SPEAKER_00]: Because some of you are maybe more senior than me in studying Buddhism, and some of you are quite new.

01:56.520 --> 02:04.223
[SPEAKER_00]: I myself usually don't travel much giving teaching or lecture in the West.

02:05.163 --> 02:11.005
[SPEAKER_00]: Mostly I'm staying in Nepal and Bhutan, taking care of the monastery.

02:14.737 --> 02:32.198
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think I spent 20 years of my life with the previous Khyentse Rinpoche, like he mentioned, since I was three, maybe, or until 25, when Khyentse Rinpoche passed away.

02:33.403 --> 02:44.994
[SPEAKER_00]: Then after that, searching for the reincarnation and preparing for the arrival of the Khyentse Rinpoche's reincarnation.

02:45.675 --> 02:51.761
[SPEAKER_00]: And once we found the reincarnation, another 20 years serving the young Khyentse Rinpoche.

02:53.142 --> 03:05.932
[SPEAKER_00]: So I feel very, very fortunate to be able to spend a long time with such a great master like Jambjeet Khyentse Rinpoche for 20 years.

03:06.753 --> 03:14.899
[SPEAKER_00]: And being with Khyentse Rinpoche that I got chance to meet so many of the great masters.

03:18.371 --> 03:28.038
[SPEAKER_00]: like His Holiness the 16th Karmapa, the 14th Dalai Lama, and many, many senior Masters, they always come and visit, or Khyentse Rinpoche go and visit them.

03:28.278 --> 03:34.103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I always feel very, very fortunate and very lucky.

03:34.963 --> 03:39.647
[SPEAKER_00]: And same many of our friends here, many of the senior students.

03:40.707 --> 03:43.850
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we should always not forget that how lucky we are.

03:44.933 --> 03:57.903
[SPEAKER_00]: to meet such great masters, realized masters who came from Tibet and then all the way to America to teach.

03:57.963 --> 04:04.148
[SPEAKER_00]: So many of us are very, very fortunate to be able to meet them.

04:04.168 --> 04:12.714
[SPEAKER_00]: I sometimes tell Western friends why Tibet had so many great masters.

04:14.201 --> 04:33.285
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because if you look at the Tibetan situation in Tibet, out of the 6 million Tibetans, more than 10% of Tibetans became monks and lamas and practicing.

04:33.705 --> 04:42.407
[SPEAKER_00]: So out of which, then Tibet produced many, many great masters, just like Brazilians.

04:43.361 --> 04:58.044
[SPEAKER_00]: out of so many population they come up the world's best 12 football player so I think in Tibet all the population is out of maybe all the population 10

04:59.105 --> 05:03.891
[SPEAKER_00]: more than 10% focusing on practicing, trying to achieve some kind of inner realization.

05:04.973 --> 05:13.204
[SPEAKER_00]: So therefore, Tibet produced not the world football champion, but the world kind of great spiritual masters.

05:15.373 --> 05:19.875
[SPEAKER_00]: So many of them came out of Tibet and came to come to West.

05:21.036 --> 05:26.059
[SPEAKER_00]: And we are very fortunate to meet them and receive teaching.

05:26.439 --> 05:34.683
[SPEAKER_00]: And some of you, many of you, I think, still continue to practice this precious lineage that we receive.

05:39.882 --> 05:48.552
[SPEAKER_00]: So to tell you a little bit about Jamsil Khyentse Rinpoche, maybe some of you didn't meet him or some of the

05:50.393 --> 05:55.336
[SPEAKER_00]: younger people maybe heard about Rinpoche or read about Khyentse Rinpoche.

05:56.597 --> 06:15.808
[SPEAKER_00]: So Khyentse Rinpoche is one of this Tibet's great masters, among the many lamas, one of the great masters who studied with 50 different teachers from the four major schools of Tibetan Buddhism.

06:17.338 --> 06:27.406
[SPEAKER_00]: and then himself not only study but he put into practice by staying more than 20 years in cave and retreat like that.

06:28.447 --> 06:38.595
[SPEAKER_00]: So Khyentse Rinpoche somehow when he left Tibet he brought the precious lineage and all the teachings.

06:40.716 --> 06:41.357
[SPEAKER_00]: So nowadays

06:42.573 --> 06:46.654
[SPEAKER_00]: Recently I met one Tibetan kind of researcher.

06:46.735 --> 06:57.278
[SPEAKER_00]: He told me about this story of one of the Khyentse Rinpoche's teachers from the Gelug school, a very well-known Geshe.

06:59.179 --> 07:09.083
[SPEAKER_00]: And this Geshe, he has predicted that Khyentse Rinpoche's Dharma activity will flourish one-third of the world.

07:11.453 --> 07:13.054
[SPEAKER_00]: I never heard that prediction before.

07:13.074 --> 07:16.877
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just recently in Queens, Jackson Heights area.

07:18.298 --> 07:21.941
[SPEAKER_00]: I heard this story.

07:22.141 --> 07:23.863
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's very true, you know.

07:24.123 --> 07:35.572
[SPEAKER_00]: I think everywhere we look, somehow Khyentse Rinpoche's lineage of teaching, and also Khyentse Rinpoche being a non-sectarian, the other day I mentioned a little bit,

07:36.610 --> 07:41.253
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a responsibility of upholding all the four schools of Tibetan Buddhist tradition.

07:42.434 --> 07:46.696
[SPEAKER_00]: So he was very much, when you travel, he traveled since 1975.

07:46.716 --> 07:54.801
[SPEAKER_00]: Wherever he traveled, he always tried to transmit whatever lineage or whatever tradition they follow.

07:55.782 --> 07:59.624
[SPEAKER_00]: Either it's from Gelug tradition, Saja tradition, then of course Nyingma and Kaji.

08:01.130 --> 08:08.915
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever tradition they follow, Rinpoche will teach according to their wishes and their kind of practice.

08:08.955 --> 08:20.643
[SPEAKER_00]: So Rinpoche, sort of, I feel that Khyentse Rinpoche is like one of the main kind of life force, can I say, of the Buddha Dharma, especially the Tibetan Buddhist tradition in the world.

08:22.344 --> 08:29.349
[SPEAKER_00]: And some of, maybe, for the young people, young children I see, if you like to know about Khyentse Rinpoche, he is physically very, very big.

08:30.788 --> 08:32.108
[SPEAKER_00]: Even in America, he's big.

08:33.489 --> 08:35.489
[SPEAKER_00]: Even when he comes to America, he's very tall.

08:36.409 --> 08:41.230
[SPEAKER_00]: At one point, one young child came to Sri Rinpoche when he was a resident.

08:41.611 --> 08:43.831
[SPEAKER_00]: And this child came with his mother.

08:45.391 --> 08:52.213
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the child saw Khyentse Rinpoche, he was so fascinated that he told his mother, look, mommy, giant man, giant man.

08:53.948 --> 08:59.150
[SPEAKER_00]: And the mother felt a little bit embarrassed because it sounds a little bit rude, you know.

08:59.810 --> 09:04.732
[SPEAKER_00]: But she couldn't make him stop because he was so fascinated with Rinpoche's physical appearance.

09:06.513 --> 09:13.616
[SPEAKER_00]: So, of course, not only inner realization, but even outer physical appearance is very, very magnificent.

09:14.276 --> 09:16.097
[SPEAKER_00]: It's almost like a mountain.

09:18.632 --> 09:19.032
[SPEAKER_00]: Like that.

09:19.192 --> 09:26.735
[SPEAKER_00]: And like in the movie, his own Dalai Lama said, his body well built, very big, but always smiling like that.

09:27.636 --> 09:29.376
[SPEAKER_00]: So his presence was very, very strong.

09:29.697 --> 09:46.283
[SPEAKER_00]: So when you meet spiritual master like that and you somehow feel spiritually connected, then I think maybe we can say kind of inner realization sort of naturally comes because the presence is so strong.

09:46.383 --> 09:46.884
[SPEAKER_00]: And many of

09:48.522 --> 09:50.563
[SPEAKER_00]: you, I'm sure, have an experience like that.

09:52.284 --> 10:03.969
[SPEAKER_00]: And today here, the subject is some poem that Khyentse Rinpoche composed.

10:06.630 --> 10:10.272
[SPEAKER_00]: So Khyentse Rinpoche always had a time for everybody.

10:11.462 --> 10:25.071
[SPEAKER_00]: So he would always write letters to his friends or his students, from very high lamas, very high-ranking masters and lamas, to a very simple, ordinary guy.

10:26.652 --> 10:29.234
[SPEAKER_00]: And he would always write them, in those days.

10:29.774 --> 10:32.636
[SPEAKER_00]: No WeChat, no email.

10:33.897 --> 10:36.239
[SPEAKER_00]: Even fax was something very modern in those days.

10:37.767 --> 10:39.187
[SPEAKER_00]: So he had time to write.

10:39.547 --> 10:45.768
[SPEAKER_00]: So when you write a letter, there's always advice how to practice.

10:46.749 --> 10:52.990
[SPEAKER_00]: And according to that, students' kind of capability, capacity.

10:53.510 --> 11:06.652
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think this, it doesn't really mention the name, but I think it was also written letter to somebody, I think, who might have asked advice on the mind teaching, I think.

11:07.692 --> 11:08.093
[SPEAKER_00]: Like that.

11:08.193 --> 11:25.227
[SPEAKER_00]: So, of course, mind teaching is very important, but I'm not talking from my experience, but since I've been with Khyentse Rinpoche since a very young age, so I hear that Khyentse Rinpoche is giving teaching.

11:25.788 --> 11:27.930
[SPEAKER_00]: I've seen how people practice and all that.

11:28.550 --> 11:35.136
[SPEAKER_00]: So personally, no experience, but I think, of course, mind teaching is the most important.

11:36.863 --> 11:50.792
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I think the essence of all the Buddha's teachings is to, how do you say, semdurva, subdue, subdue the mind.

11:57.463 --> 12:05.205
[SPEAKER_00]: Since sometimes we say in Tibetan, something so close, something so near, that you won't see like your eyelashes.

12:06.225 --> 12:14.928
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know whether in the West you use this quote or not, but in Tibetan we say, because our eyelashes are so close, so we don't really see that, see them.

12:15.948 --> 12:24.670
[SPEAKER_00]: So you need a kind of, in order to really get the teaching of mind normally,

12:26.093 --> 12:32.076
[SPEAKER_00]: People will go through lots of practice, preliminary practice, nondra practice.

12:33.477 --> 12:47.864
[SPEAKER_00]: And from the nondra, I think to become a really, to do a good practice or good practice, to become a good practitioner, I think that why we practice, I think that's very important.

12:49.020 --> 12:56.142
[SPEAKER_00]: not just because it's fashionable, or not just because it sounds good, or not just because it's kind of temporary relaxing.

12:57.003 --> 13:08.427
[SPEAKER_00]: The purpose why we want to practice dharma has to be, the reason why we want to practice has to be, because of what we call the four changes of mind, or four thoughts.

13:10.427 --> 13:12.308
[SPEAKER_00]: Four thoughts that turn the mind into dharma.

13:13.848 --> 13:15.489
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, precious human life,

13:18.690 --> 13:21.833
[SPEAKER_00]: so much potential that we have.

13:23.294 --> 13:33.483
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, of course, in the traditional, classic teaching, there is all this reflection.

13:33.603 --> 13:40.989
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, in the traditional teaching, the five things,

13:47.836 --> 13:48.697
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, 18...

13:49.477 --> 13:52.499
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how to say it in English.

13:55.740 --> 14:02.464
[SPEAKER_00]: So all these things are to reflect, to see how precious human life is.

14:02.484 --> 14:10.268
[SPEAKER_00]: So sometimes we can also use from the modern kind of thinking, that if you look at the evolution of the...

14:10.979 --> 14:23.937
[SPEAKER_00]: modern science, that how the human race started from being a cell, slowly to a fish, and then slowly, slowly to, you know, I'm sure more of you study science.

14:24.915 --> 14:32.558
[SPEAKER_00]: And then when I sometimes think, there are like millions of chance that we not become a human being, that we become a mushroom or we become a tree.

14:35.299 --> 14:41.242
[SPEAKER_00]: So, of course, mushroom and tree don't have the intelligence that we have.

14:43.242 --> 14:55.028
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think from that evolution point of view, I think we can also look at how precious human life is, because we have so much intelligence, so much potential.

14:57.068 --> 15:07.513
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, also, people who have this precious human life, how many of us really utilize it properly?

15:11.771 --> 15:30.395
[SPEAKER_00]: many people somehow take it for granted as a kind of a joke one Tibetan Lama was teaching this precious human life to a Chinese student and he said all the precious human life is very rare and the Chinese student said the Lama you've never been to China

15:37.183 --> 15:38.604
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not like that, I think.

15:38.905 --> 15:43.949
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just being a human, but someone who wants to give some meaning to one's life.

15:45.130 --> 15:45.631
[SPEAKER_00]: Very rare.

15:46.351 --> 15:49.654
[SPEAKER_00]: And those who want to give meaning to one's life, finding the right path.

15:52.218 --> 16:00.065
[SPEAKER_00]: I think those things feel, by thinking or contemplating those things, then we feel how precious human life.

16:01.145 --> 16:02.246
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is number one.

16:02.667 --> 16:12.535
[SPEAKER_00]: And then number two, I think what we call the impermanence, contemplating on impermanence, everything changes, nothing is permanent.

16:14.438 --> 16:23.896
[SPEAKER_00]: And impermanence is not always bad news, because many people think impermanence is bad news, but impermanence is the nature of how things are.

16:24.818 --> 16:26.681
[SPEAKER_00]: Rich become poor, poor become rich,

16:28.100 --> 16:31.602
[SPEAKER_00]: lazy become diligent, diligent become lazy, everything changes.

16:31.622 --> 16:33.543
[SPEAKER_00]: So that is how the things are.

16:33.563 --> 16:45.009
[SPEAKER_00]: So contemplating on impermanence, I think even in sutras and many teachings, it says that one of the most important, and especially for the people who wants to practice dharma.

16:46.390 --> 16:49.892
[SPEAKER_00]: Then believing in understanding law of the cause and effect.

16:53.379 --> 16:56.480
[SPEAKER_00]: everything has a cause, everything has an effect.

16:56.520 --> 17:06.546
[SPEAKER_00]: So believing, having believing marita, ichi, trust, trust in Allah, then the suffering of the samsara.

17:08.867 --> 17:09.668
[SPEAKER_00]: So all those things.

17:13.150 --> 17:25.457
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the suffering of samsara we all gathered here, we have some sense of understanding of that, then we look for some kind of way to be out of the suffering of the samsara.

17:26.037 --> 17:36.783
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think those four changes of mind, even though we heard this so many times, or even though it sounds quite kind of easy intellectually,

17:37.867 --> 17:42.918
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we really have to put it into our heart and it has to come from our heart.

17:46.329 --> 17:56.714
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the story in Badr Rinpoche, when he was teaching the four thoughts to his students.

17:57.795 --> 18:05.679
[SPEAKER_00]: And Badr Rinpoche is one of the great Tibetan yogi masters.

18:05.759 --> 18:12.683
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the best example of the practitioner who lived somewhere near Dzogchen monastery.

18:13.895 --> 18:18.908
[SPEAKER_00]: And Rinpoche would stay in the cave and the students would also stay in the cave.

18:20.310 --> 18:28.234
[SPEAKER_00]: And then when there is no appointment or there is no timing, when the time is right, the master would come out and call for the student, hey, where you are?

18:28.954 --> 18:33.756
[SPEAKER_00]: And the student will come up from the bush or wherever he was meditating.

18:35.497 --> 18:44.621
[SPEAKER_00]: If you've been to Tibet or you have seen the Tibetans, then you can imagine, especially in Shechen area, it's a big cloud like that coming out.

18:45.621 --> 18:46.902
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would sit on the rock.

18:48.897 --> 18:53.138
[SPEAKER_00]: And Badr Rinpoche would say one sentence.

18:56.139 --> 18:58.660
[SPEAKER_00]: The precious human life is very rare.

19:00.681 --> 19:03.301
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the teacher would cry.

19:03.782 --> 19:04.862
[SPEAKER_00]: And the student would cry.

19:05.682 --> 19:07.003
[SPEAKER_00]: So then they cry, cry, cry.

19:07.223 --> 19:08.023
[SPEAKER_00]: The teaching finished.

19:13.484 --> 19:16.165
[SPEAKER_00]: So then next day, again, the lama called.

19:16.185 --> 19:17.046
[SPEAKER_00]: The student comes.

19:17.286 --> 19:17.826
[SPEAKER_00]: So like that.

19:18.708 --> 19:21.149
[SPEAKER_00]: So sometimes we don't need too much blah blah blah talking.

19:22.210 --> 19:26.892
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the main thing, I think it has to come really from the heart.

19:28.573 --> 19:28.933
[SPEAKER_00]: Like that.

19:30.254 --> 19:39.798
[SPEAKER_00]: So these things are, I think, very important in order to root the dharma into ourselves.

19:39.838 --> 19:40.939
[SPEAKER_00]: Can we say root?

19:41.239 --> 19:43.040
[SPEAKER_00]: Rooting the dharma into ourselves.

19:43.923 --> 19:58.729
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think in the West now, it's not really new, maybe almost 50 years since Buddha Dharma came to the West, and I'm sure now the Dharma is really rooted in the Western students' minds.

20:04.631 --> 20:11.413
[SPEAKER_00]: When we first came with the robes, walking around in New York, more people thought we were Hare Krishna.

20:13.565 --> 20:21.951
[SPEAKER_00]: And when Khyentse Rinpoche was coming out, I remember the immigration officer says, look, big Hare Krishna is coming.

20:22.772 --> 20:26.395
[SPEAKER_00]: So those days, Buddha Dharma not well known, not much known in the West.

20:27.776 --> 20:30.598
[SPEAKER_00]: And now maybe the Hare Krishna's people might say, oh, Buddhist monk.

20:32.776 --> 20:33.336
[SPEAKER_00]: So like that.

20:33.997 --> 20:34.677
[SPEAKER_00]: So gradually.

20:35.037 --> 20:45.363
[SPEAKER_00]: And those days, of course, many of the senior students, you might remember that many kind of people looking for some kind of Asian exotic, you know, things like that.

20:45.443 --> 20:53.067
[SPEAKER_00]: And many students are like hippies, don't want to do much, looking for some kind of levitation or something like that.

20:53.087 --> 20:53.988
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's how it started.

20:54.068 --> 20:59.191
[SPEAKER_00]: And masters, our senior masters really work hard, really work hard.

21:01.292 --> 21:02.673
[SPEAKER_00]: to bring the Dharma in.

21:03.774 --> 21:11.301
[SPEAKER_00]: I was joking with my friend, one of the Rinpoche who came around the same time of Shakyam Trungpa Rinpoche and those Chimmy Rinpoche.

21:11.321 --> 21:14.544
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, those days you have advantage and disadvantage.

21:16.566 --> 21:22.110
[SPEAKER_00]: Disadvantage is you have to teach from zero to the Western students because they really don't know anything.

21:23.191 --> 21:26.494
[SPEAKER_00]: And the advantage is you could have said anything nobody would have known.

21:30.025 --> 21:55.715
[SPEAKER_00]: and now it's not like that everybody can google and then say so like that now i think dharma is in the west for a long time and i think that really the buddha dharma is not just a kind of super superficial many of the students really serious so many of you really i think this kind of four changes the purpose giving way to give meaning to life

21:56.490 --> 21:58.131
[SPEAKER_00]: really is rooted into your heart.

21:58.712 --> 22:02.935
[SPEAKER_00]: And then now we already have second, third generation Buddhist followers.

22:05.056 --> 22:25.490
[SPEAKER_00]: And then mind teaching is given to sometimes some people who would ask, who are too old to go through the nyondros, or too, how do you say, did a long time retreat, and then now they want kind of final advice.

22:27.188 --> 22:55.918
[SPEAKER_00]: from teacher then they would ask just simple mind teaching like that and then teacher would say few words and then the student will go and practice come back report and again like that again few words and go and practice and come back and report so that's how normally taught the mind teaching

22:56.442 --> 23:08.845
[SPEAKER_00]: And it is also, the introduction to the mind is not really kind of hidden somewhere that only gives to some students.

23:09.925 --> 23:24.889
[SPEAKER_00]: The teaching is always there in the teachings, even like there are certain texts that we recite, Guru Rinpoche's Sondip Aspiration, Supplication Prayer.

23:26.180 --> 23:33.608
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's a very, very clearly mentioned this kind of practice, but only sometime then we sort of just read it through.

23:33.728 --> 23:36.811
[SPEAKER_00]: We don't really pay attention or maybe we are not ready to understand.

23:37.832 --> 23:45.740
[SPEAKER_00]: Like example in the seven branch, you know, seven chapter Guru Rinpoche prayer.

23:51.616 --> 23:57.440
[SPEAKER_00]: So whatever appearance appears, just let it appear without clinging to it.

23:58.120 --> 24:01.443
[SPEAKER_00]: Let it subside.

24:01.803 --> 24:03.504
[SPEAKER_00]: So this prayer is always there.

24:04.225 --> 24:08.988
[SPEAKER_00]: And also many of you received empowerment during the fourth empowerment.

24:09.368 --> 24:12.734
[SPEAKER_00]: there is always an introduction to the mind, always there.

24:13.375 --> 24:23.030
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think this is not something that is only given to some people, it is always given, but I think the special kind of what we call

24:24.892 --> 24:40.275
[SPEAKER_00]: busy in session or whatever some when the time is right and I think master says few things and then you somehow actualize or get it there are some stories again with the pattern which I connected with pattern which I

24:41.305 --> 24:50.367
[SPEAKER_00]: that some incidents that Padurumbache's teacher, he sort of hit him on the head and call him a mad dog and then that sort of wake him up.

24:52.807 --> 24:56.468
[SPEAKER_00]: Not always hitting, some other cases no hitting also.

24:58.548 --> 25:07.370
[SPEAKER_00]: Padurumbache and his student Nyosho Lungtok sitting in the meadow of Dzogchen near this snow mountain.

25:08.711 --> 25:14.676
[SPEAKER_00]: And Bhattacharya Rinpoche, I think it must be in the evening, he said, Can you see the stars in the sky?

25:15.636 --> 25:16.737
[SPEAKER_00]: Then his students said, Yes.

25:18.479 --> 25:21.181
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you hear the dogs barking in the village?

25:21.942 --> 25:23.163
[SPEAKER_00]: The students said, Yes.

25:24.244 --> 25:25.845
[SPEAKER_00]: So what is the nature of your mind?

25:27.286 --> 25:28.827
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all it takes.

25:30.689 --> 25:32.470
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think a lot of maturation.

25:35.004 --> 25:38.266
[SPEAKER_00]: Preparation, preparation, maturation, I think.

25:39.146 --> 25:44.269
[SPEAKER_00]: Then we will kind of actualize or realize this kind of thing.

25:45.210 --> 25:50.433
[SPEAKER_00]: So speaking too much, I think, doesn't really make much difference.

25:50.493 --> 25:53.834
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like maybe I'm telling you how to ride a bicycle.

25:53.854 --> 25:58.837
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like that.

25:59.738 --> 26:03.760
[SPEAKER_00]: So here in the poem that Khyentse Rinpoche wrote,

26:09.039 --> 26:15.843
[SPEAKER_00]: So here I will ask Sean to translate, so if I make a mistake I can blame him.

26:33.827 --> 26:35.629
[SPEAKER_00]: When we think of the human experience,

26:53.017 --> 26:58.380
[SPEAKER_01]: We are a composite of body, mind, and with speech running between the two.

26:59.281 --> 27:04.584
[SPEAKER_01]: And from within these three, body, speech, and mind, mind is the most important.

27:05.605 --> 27:16.431
[SPEAKER_01]: And with regard to the subduing of the mind, or the subjugation of the unruly emotions and thoughts, et cetera, that run wild in our mind, the Buddha gave numerous teachings.

27:20.214 --> 27:21.875
[SPEAKER_00]: KHENPO KARTEN RINPOCHE KARTEN RINPOCHE

27:22.920 --> 27:22.940
[SPEAKER_00]: .

27:22.960 --> 27:23.040
[SPEAKER_00]: .

27:23.100 --> 27:23.180
[SPEAKER_00]: .

27:23.200 --> 27:23.220
[SPEAKER_00]: .

27:23.260 --> 27:23.280
[SPEAKER_00]: .

27:46.902 --> 27:58.084
[SPEAKER_01]: So as I mentioned, the Buddha gave innumerable teachings, all of which have the sole purpose of subduing or subjugating the unruly mind.

28:00.014 --> 28:14.524
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if one were to kind of put all of these together or to combine all of these in a very simple way, then one shouldn't look outside of oneself for a method or a means, independence upon which one can subdue the mind.

28:14.584 --> 28:17.026
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather, one should look within oneself.

28:18.046 --> 28:22.749
[SPEAKER_01]: So here, then, the first line of the poem reads, when mind is watching mind.

28:23.330 --> 28:27.653
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, rather than looking outside of oneself, one must choose to look within.

28:28.877 --> 28:33.881
[SPEAKER_01]: One will find then that mind and the one who is supposedly watching mind are not separate.

28:34.261 --> 28:35.802
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather, they are one and the same.

28:37.103 --> 28:39.845
[SPEAKER_00]: So when one kind of looks within,

29:00.100 --> 29:03.081
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, looking at the mind, there is nothing to see.

29:04.241 --> 29:05.121
[SPEAKER_01]: It is vividly clear.

29:06.301 --> 29:14.663
[SPEAKER_01]: So when one tries to look at mind, there is nothing, there is no color, neither is there a shape upon which to focus.

29:15.303 --> 29:18.284
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather, when mind is watching mind, there is nothing to see.

29:19.284 --> 29:23.125
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't that there is something obscured or that there is something hidden away.

29:23.285 --> 29:27.346
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather, the nature of one's mind is something that is vividly clear.

29:29.040 --> 29:29.060
[SPEAKER_00]: .

29:29.100 --> 29:29.160
[SPEAKER_00]: .

29:29.180 --> 29:29.200
[SPEAKER_00]: .

29:29.260 --> 29:29.340
[SPEAKER_00]: .

29:29.360 --> 29:29.380
[SPEAKER_00]: .

29:50.215 --> 29:51.136
[SPEAKER_01]: So how to do that?

29:51.877 --> 29:57.522
[SPEAKER_01]: How to then kind of rest within the nature of mind and see then this natural luminosity of mind?

29:58.323 --> 30:02.948
[SPEAKER_01]: We're advised then in the third line that one should rest naturally.

30:03.068 --> 30:04.570
[SPEAKER_01]: And how should that rest occur?

30:05.130 --> 30:06.451
[SPEAKER_01]: It should be uncontrived.

30:07.112 --> 30:15.396
[SPEAKER_01]: Here then, one shouldn't confuse oneself, nor should one kind of stir up the mind, but rather one should simply allow things to settle.

30:15.976 --> 30:24.740
[SPEAKER_01]: One shouldn't give rise to an emotion, neither should one give rise to a chain of thoughts, but rather remain, without contriving the mind, free and at ease.

30:37.499 --> 30:46.502
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, when one allows thought to rest naturally and everything becomes still, becomes quiet then, one should be alert.

30:47.422 --> 31:04.347
[SPEAKER_01]: If distraction occurs, if one becomes distracted, if thought arises that would kind of ruin one's meditation, one should recognize that and simply let it go and remain, as is written here, naturally, resting naturally and without distraction.

31:06.942 --> 31:12.225
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think when we do this kind of looking into your mind,

31:13.280 --> 31:28.185
[SPEAKER_00]: I think first thing I think we have to forget about all the intellectual understanding of what is mind or philosophical ideas that we have put into our brain or even a scientific ideas of how the brain and all the cells work.

31:28.866 --> 31:35.128
[SPEAKER_00]: So because this thing, this intellectual understanding can affect the actual understanding of the mind, I think.

31:35.748 --> 31:41.110
[SPEAKER_00]: So therefore you should be a little bit like a young child or forget all these things.

31:42.824 --> 31:51.628
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't realize, but I was told by Jyotish Khyentse Rinpoche to get the mind instruction from his only 16 kamapa.

31:52.829 --> 31:56.630
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was only about seven years old, seven or six years old.

31:57.631 --> 31:59.311
[SPEAKER_00]: And they would sit next to each other.

31:59.932 --> 32:05.614
[SPEAKER_00]: And then kamapa would tell me, OK, tomorrow you have to tell me what your mind looks like.

32:06.775 --> 32:11.557
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I go and think all night, oh, my mind is like a mountain.

32:12.915 --> 32:14.756
[SPEAKER_00]: I go back and I report next day.

32:15.316 --> 32:16.917
[SPEAKER_00]: And both of them, ah, ha, ha, ha.

32:20.439 --> 32:24.442
[SPEAKER_00]: So they're sort of waiting for my next reply.

32:25.622 --> 32:27.644
[SPEAKER_00]: Every time I come with very strange ideas.

32:29.465 --> 32:33.307
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think, like child's mind, we don't have so much concept.

32:34.287 --> 32:35.688
[SPEAKER_00]: Sort of just pure.

32:36.569 --> 32:37.669
[SPEAKER_00]: Then look, look back.

32:43.669 --> 32:43.749
[SPEAKER_00]: .

32:43.769 --> 32:43.829
[SPEAKER_00]: .

32:43.949 --> 32:44.049
[SPEAKER_00]: .

32:44.069 --> 32:44.089
[SPEAKER_00]: .

32:58.840 --> 33:06.361
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever thought occurs from that state without stopping or analyzing, watch its very nature.

33:07.322 --> 33:10.162
[SPEAKER_01]: Its arising doesn't obscure the absolute nature.

33:11.022 --> 33:14.023
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever occurs, relax right there.

33:15.343 --> 33:26.085
[SPEAKER_01]: Here then, when we are trying to remain within then the natural state, the uncontrived natural state, then a thought occurs, if a motion erupts, if you will,

33:26.585 --> 33:31.631
[SPEAKER_01]: At that time, it is imperative to initially recognize that a thought occurrence.

33:43.106 --> 33:50.012
[SPEAKER_01]: When the thought arises, as you read in the second line, one shouldn't stop it, neither should one analyze it.

33:50.832 --> 33:54.115
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather, when the thought arises, one should recognize it.

33:54.895 --> 33:58.678
[SPEAKER_01]: Recognizing the thought, then, one should turn one's attention towards its nature.

33:59.539 --> 34:04.522
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, relax or rest then within the empty nature of mind.

34:04.622 --> 34:08.704
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, without stopping or analyzing, watch the thought's very nature.

34:08.724 --> 34:08.744
[SPEAKER_01]: .

34:30.403 --> 34:40.577
[SPEAKER_01]: So the arising of thought within the experience of resting within the uncontrived nature then, the arising of thought doesn't obscure the absolute nature of mind.

34:42.119 --> 34:44.081
[SPEAKER_01]: By recognizing thought,

34:45.092 --> 35:01.761
[SPEAKER_01]: thinking of looking into its nature then, whatever occurs, whatever thought manifests, whatever emotion it might be, recognizing the nature of that thought or emotion then, one should simply relax into its nature and naturally the thought will subside.

35:03.121 --> 35:09.564
[SPEAKER_00]: So, quite often, Khyentse Rinpoche used to give an example of throwing a stone at the dog.

35:09.924 --> 35:13.526
[SPEAKER_00]: If you throw a stone at a dog, then the dog will chase the stone.

35:14.266 --> 35:17.967
[SPEAKER_00]: So when a dog chases a stone, you can throw many more stones at the dog.

35:19.508 --> 35:23.590
[SPEAKER_00]: Then if you throw a stone at a lion, the lion will look back at you.

35:24.913 --> 35:26.633
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you cannot throw the second stone.

35:28.014 --> 35:38.056
[SPEAKER_00]: So like that, I think when one thought arises, if you follow the thought, then one thought will chain reaction, lead to another thought, like many, many thoughts.

35:38.816 --> 35:45.197
[SPEAKER_00]: So when the one thought, like one stone to the lion, and lion look back, and then the thought will sort of dissolve, disappear.

35:52.007 --> 35:54.848
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't follow thoughts about the past.

35:55.508 --> 35:58.269
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't anticipate thoughts about the future.

36:21.587 --> 36:23.792
[SPEAKER_01]: directly transcend the external world.

36:24.934 --> 36:27.018
[SPEAKER_01]: That is called the four parts without three.

36:31.465 --> 36:39.147
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, one shouldn't dwell in the past, thinking about things that one has done in whatever form that may take.

36:40.127 --> 36:50.349
[SPEAKER_01]: Similarly then, one shouldn't anticipate the future, thinking about making too many plans and reflecting on what one should or shouldn't do at a particular time in the future, etc.

36:51.010 --> 36:52.670
[SPEAKER_01]: One is advised to avoid these two.

36:56.555 --> 36:56.956
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

37:02.045 --> 37:03.486
[SPEAKER_00]: And to then directly transcend the external world.

37:03.526 --> 37:11.750
[SPEAKER_00]: So here when we reflect using the three times, then we've spoken about not dwelling in the past, and similarly then about anticipating the future.

37:28.038 --> 37:37.031
[SPEAKER_01]: Directly transcending the external world means to not become involved in that which is in front of us at the very moment in any given time.

37:37.111 --> 37:41.938
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, we think about overcoming the thoughts of the three times.

37:42.438 --> 37:44.661
[SPEAKER_01]: This then is the fourth part without three.

37:46.967 --> 37:46.987
[SPEAKER_00]: .

37:47.027 --> 37:47.067
[SPEAKER_00]: .

37:47.087 --> 37:47.107
[SPEAKER_00]: .

37:47.147 --> 37:47.167
[SPEAKER_00]: .

37:47.187 --> 37:47.207
[SPEAKER_00]: .

38:07.301 --> 38:14.985
[SPEAKER_01]: If you maintain the recognition of thoughts, you will feel that they do not truly begin, remain or end.

38:16.026 --> 38:19.348
[SPEAKER_01]: Though you notice them, they have no effect on your true nature.

38:20.488 --> 38:23.190
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the bare natural state, the way it is.

38:24.662 --> 38:40.592
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, when we think about the recognition of thoughts, here then, it's essential that we recognize that the kind of empty nature of thoughts, as such, really, when we look at a thought, it doesn't truly begin or come into existence.

38:49.255 --> 38:49.335
[SPEAKER_00]: .

38:49.355 --> 38:49.375
[SPEAKER_00]: .

38:49.395 --> 38:49.475
[SPEAKER_00]: .

38:49.515 --> 38:49.595
[SPEAKER_00]: .

38:49.615 --> 38:49.635
[SPEAKER_00]: .

38:59.776 --> 39:09.605
[SPEAKER_01]: So for example, if we take a crystal and we put it in sunlight, for example, then almost instantaneously rainbow colors will reflect from the stone.

39:10.826 --> 39:19.773
[SPEAKER_01]: Similarly then, when we think about mind, when it is put before various objects, et cetera, it is almost as if instantaneously thoughts arise.

39:20.314 --> 39:24.397
[SPEAKER_01]: However, when the condition for their arisal, in the example of the crystal, the light,

39:25.118 --> 39:45.264
[SPEAKER_01]: is absent the red the the rainbow-colored light is naturally absent as well so similarly when the object that gives or the idea to give rise to a continuity of thought is missing then it is as if that the thought was were never there it's not an inherent or integral part of the nature of one's mind

39:53.382 --> 39:54.023
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you very much.

40:09.237 --> 40:15.160
[SPEAKER_01]: So the arisal of a particular thought is not an integral part or an inherent part.

40:15.840 --> 40:20.662
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not one and the same, if you will, with the natural state of mind itself.

40:22.043 --> 40:34.468
[SPEAKER_01]: So if a thought isn't an inherently existent part of one's mind, then its ending or its cessation can similarly not be an integral or inherent part of mind.

40:35.189 --> 40:37.390
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, what you talk about here, it's remaining.

40:38.525 --> 40:41.607
[SPEAKER_01]: So then the question is, do thoughts not exist at all?

40:42.087 --> 40:44.428
[SPEAKER_01]: They exist, but they don't truly exist.

40:55.976 --> 41:06.022
[SPEAKER_00]: and so for example when we think about then the way there is no inherent kind of arising or cessation of thoughts as integral to them the nature of one's mind

41:19.351 --> 41:35.868
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, with regard to mind itself, mind itself cannot be neither benefited nor harmed by the arisal or the cessation of these seemingly tangible, seemingly integral parts of our being, or these thoughts, if you will.

41:36.548 --> 41:43.132
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, we can notice the thoughts arising then, but then they will have no effect on our true nature.

41:43.332 --> 41:48.955
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, the advice is given then to simply rest in the true nature of mind.

42:04.924 --> 42:09.405
[SPEAKER_01]: This then is the experience of the bare natural state, the way it is now.

42:10.345 --> 42:28.850
[SPEAKER_01]: If you take that empty awareness that is open and carefree as the path, all the time, both during formal meditation practice and also afterward in the post-meditation time, you will quickly and surely acquire a confident realization that confusion is freed by itself.

42:31.078 --> 42:31.098
[SPEAKER_00]: .

42:56.152 --> 42:59.214
[SPEAKER_01]: So here then, there are two things to bear in mind.

43:00.095 --> 43:15.605
[SPEAKER_01]: So the empty kind of awareness, that is then the formal kind of meditation practice, if you will, simply to remain within that bare natural state the way it is, open and carefree, and that's our kind of spiritual path, if you will.

43:16.863 --> 43:20.358
[SPEAKER_01]: But when it comes to post meditation period,

43:21.110 --> 43:37.198
[SPEAKER_01]: the afterward, if you will, of the formal meditation practice, then it is essential to, at all times and in all situations, as much as one is possible, to look at them, the nature of mind, when thoughts arise, to simply look at their nature.

43:37.799 --> 43:44.322
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is similar then to the formal meditation practice where one sustains the empty awareness, the nature of the mind.

43:44.642 --> 43:50.085
[SPEAKER_01]: So as such, during the post-meditation period, as much as one is able, one looks at the nature

43:50.986 --> 43:52.027
[SPEAKER_01]: of one's mind.

43:52.909 --> 43:55.733
[SPEAKER_00]: So something to do when you get stuck in the New York traffic.

44:07.176 --> 44:18.519
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'm not really joking here.

44:19.339 --> 44:35.043
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're stuck in the New York traffic, or when you're in an elevator, or when you're on a bus, when you're traveling on an airplane, et cetera, it's very easy simply to look within, to look at the mind.

44:36.263 --> 44:48.532
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, when I say kind of meditate like this, to look within, to look at the nature of mind, I'm not suggesting that you kind of have to try to kind of sit cross-legged and kind of straighten your spine and put your hands in the meditation posture, etc.

44:48.552 --> 44:49.092
[SPEAKER_01]: Not at all.

44:49.453 --> 44:51.354
[SPEAKER_01]: Here one just simply looks within.

45:05.212 --> 45:14.682
[SPEAKER_00]: And as such, mention is made here then, that if you continue this practice as much as you're able, putting a great deal of effort into it,

45:28.134 --> 45:36.640
[SPEAKER_01]: then you will quickly and surely acquire a confident realization that confusion then is freed by itself.

45:36.720 --> 45:43.625
[SPEAKER_01]: So the confusion then of grasping at the seemingly true existence of things will naturally be set free by itself.

45:52.858 --> 45:57.962
[SPEAKER_01]: It is very helpful then, we read, to mingle your mind inseparably with your teachers.

45:59.303 --> 46:06.909
[SPEAKER_01]: By settling in equipoise within that state, developing faith and devotion, you will meet the natural face of Dharmakaya.

46:09.473 --> 46:17.996
[SPEAKER_00]: I always tell some kind of newcomer who never had an opportunity to meet my lineage.

46:18.096 --> 46:20.196
[SPEAKER_00]: From my lineage, it's like Jyotish Khyentse Rinpoche.

46:20.336 --> 46:23.678
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, different lineages, they all have very realized masters.

46:24.678 --> 46:27.539
[SPEAKER_00]: So those who didn't have an opportunity to meet those masters.

46:28.279 --> 46:33.781
[SPEAKER_00]: So I always say, when you say this makes ...

46:36.382 --> 46:36.942
[SPEAKER_00]: Mingle your mind.

46:36.962 --> 46:38.063
[SPEAKER_00]: Mingle your mind with Guru.

46:38.343 --> 46:38.683
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

46:39.404 --> 46:44.267
[SPEAKER_00]: To my people who come to me and my friends, I always recommend Jyotish Khyentse Rinpoche.

46:44.947 --> 46:47.809
[SPEAKER_00]: Because physically now, not there.

46:48.609 --> 46:54.193
[SPEAKER_00]: But like in the movie, he's on the Dalai Lama set, spiritual energy or whatever, the lineage is very much alive.

46:55.333 --> 46:59.736
[SPEAKER_00]: So we can always have this kind of Guru.

46:59.836 --> 47:02.778
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think it's really necessary to have someone who's physically there.

47:03.636 --> 47:17.203
[SPEAKER_00]: So to give teaching, to transmit lineage, okay, younger lamas, lineage holders can do, but the root guru or source of inspiration from my lineage, like Chabji Khyentse Rinpoche, I always say.

47:18.864 --> 47:28.750
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, nowadays, thanks to the modern technology, we have a lot of videos, we have photos, we know how they look like, we know how they sound like, you know.

47:30.150 --> 47:32.752
[SPEAKER_00]: So very much, we can still connect.

47:34.318 --> 47:37.361
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone like Longchen Ba, Jimmy Lingba, or these great masters.

47:37.862 --> 47:42.446
[SPEAKER_00]: We don't have much idea how, unless looking at the painting Thangka.

47:43.006 --> 47:44.788
[SPEAKER_00]: And Thangka also, each one looks different.

47:47.070 --> 47:50.093
[SPEAKER_00]: So now we have these photos and videos.

47:50.153 --> 47:51.014
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's very helpful.

47:52.095 --> 47:55.178
[SPEAKER_00]: So, spiritually, still not life, doesn't matter, I think.

47:55.778 --> 48:00.182
[SPEAKER_00]: Like His Holiness the Dalai Lama said, spiritual energy is very much life, lineage is very much life.

48:00.783 --> 48:05.246
[SPEAKER_00]: You can get the instructions, teachings, empowerment, the source of inspiration, like Khyentse Rinpoche.

48:07.768 --> 48:29.963
[SPEAKER_00]: I think also in Nyingma and Gajju tradition, maybe in other traditions also, but always there is end of the guru yoga, the dissolving the three syllable letters from guru's forehead, guru's throat, guru's heart, syllable letter OM AH HUNG radiates three lights dissolving into one's forehead, throat and heart center.

48:30.784 --> 48:35.607
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it purifies our body, speech, mind, accumulating, purifies.

48:36.516 --> 48:44.500
[SPEAKER_00]: And then at the end, the three lights come, dissolving into one's forehead, throat, and heart, and purifies...

48:45.660 --> 48:46.521
[SPEAKER_00]: Subtle obscurations.

48:46.581 --> 48:47.501
[SPEAKER_00]: Subtle obscurations.

48:48.121 --> 48:49.942
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Guru dissolves into one's heart.

48:50.663 --> 48:55.445
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's where, I think, what we're describing here, mingle one's mind.

48:56.185 --> 49:09.347
[SPEAKER_00]: So a little understanding, if you can gain from some practice or lots of meditating, then in that moment, I think this is the time to sort of look at the mind.

49:10.027 --> 49:19.809
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you have some experience, then understand that is what we call the Yidam Guru.

49:21.049 --> 49:25.250
[SPEAKER_00]: So within this, you naturally feel very strong devotion.

49:27.187 --> 49:37.835
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is the ultimate dharmakaya guru, seeing the face of the dharmakaya.

49:39.477 --> 49:40.337
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, that was in English.

49:41.138 --> 49:43.600
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry.

49:44.821 --> 49:45.241
[SPEAKER_01]: Excuse me.

49:46.802 --> 49:47.703
[SPEAKER_01]: Shall I say it in Tibetan?

49:47.863 --> 49:47.983
[SPEAKER_01]: OK.

49:58.235 --> 50:03.338
[SPEAKER_00]: So we should never carry away in a kind of high view.

50:03.358 --> 50:06.760
[SPEAKER_00]: We should hear in the last sentence, always.

50:20.670 --> 50:32.016
[SPEAKER_00]: meditate on impermanence, have renunciation, and especially the conduct, law of the conduct, law of the karma, sultan karmic law.

50:32.377 --> 50:32.657
[SPEAKER_00]: Effort.

50:32.937 --> 50:33.257
[SPEAKER_00]: Effort.

50:33.497 --> 50:39.601
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are an ordained monk or nun, then very strictly follow the Vinaya law of conduct.

50:40.201 --> 50:44.243
[SPEAKER_00]: Then if you are a lay practitioner, then the law of the karma we have to follow.

50:44.864 --> 50:46.725
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes the view is very high,

50:47.485 --> 50:51.267
[SPEAKER_00]: and then we jump in the view, and then we fall down.

50:53.027 --> 50:58.690
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think the view has to be vast, and our conduct has to be very thoroughly verified.

51:12.725 --> 51:19.210
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, especially guard the continuity of impermanence, renunciation, and pure discipline like your own eyes.

51:19.991 --> 51:26.576
[SPEAKER_01]: Never diminish your one-pointed diligence in the heart of the matter, the essential yoga of profound meaning.

51:27.236 --> 51:40.647
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, it is imperative at all times and in all situations to apply single-pointed diligence at the heart of the matter to actualize this essential yoga of profound meaning.

51:42.169 --> 51:44.011
[SPEAKER_00]: So Khenpo always emphasized on

51:55.388 --> 51:57.309
[SPEAKER_00]: Tövbe means hearing the Dharma.

51:57.650 --> 51:59.691
[SPEAKER_00]: Samba means contemplating.

52:00.692 --> 52:01.752
[SPEAKER_00]: Gomba means meditation.

52:03.113 --> 52:07.456
[SPEAKER_00]: So always Khyentse Rinpoche recommends here also like that.

52:08.237 --> 52:12.800
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we should listen to Dharma, contemplate it, and then try to put into practice.

52:13.901 --> 52:22.927
[SPEAKER_00]: So if we just listen to so many dharmas and then we don't contemplate, we don't think about, then it becomes my diet book.

52:24.976 --> 52:31.079
[SPEAKER_00]: I have so many diet books and I'm still quite heavy.

52:33.860 --> 52:38.263
[SPEAKER_00]: This, I think, happens quite a lot with some of what we call dharma bumps.

52:38.803 --> 52:46.567
[SPEAKER_00]: We collect so many books and attend all the teachings and our character never changes.

52:47.827 --> 52:49.068
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it's like my diet book.

52:50.400 --> 52:57.004
[SPEAKER_00]: So maybe you don't need too many diet books, but one good diet book, and if you follow it, you will lose weight.

53:01.147 --> 53:01.687
[SPEAKER_00]: So like that.

53:20.607 --> 53:31.473
[SPEAKER_01]: May this delightful sacred offering cloud of essential pit instructions cause you to gain realization of the definitive secret that is like space.

53:32.054 --> 53:38.337
[SPEAKER_01]: So here then, this is described as the kind of quintessence of the Dharma, the essential pit instructions.

53:40.238 --> 53:44.461
[SPEAKER_00]: I think Rinpoche is writing this to some kind of higher Lama.

53:45.181 --> 53:47.162
[SPEAKER_00]: The way he describes it, it's like an offering.

53:52.012 --> 54:12.606
[SPEAKER_01]: So if you put this into practice, then this particular instruction, if engaged diligently, will cause you then to gain realization of the definitive secret, which is likened here to space.

54:15.128 --> 54:16.169
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think that's it.

54:21.393 --> 54:25.015
[SPEAKER_00]: So what is it that you recognize when you recognize the nature of the mind?

54:25.355 --> 54:26.096
[SPEAKER_00]: That's her question.

54:35.061 --> 54:36.562
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know whether I have a ...

54:38.096 --> 54:39.657
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you call it?

54:40.237 --> 54:40.757
[SPEAKER_00]: Actualize?

54:41.737 --> 54:42.158
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognize.

54:42.478 --> 54:49.560
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognize or not, that is up to my teacher to decide.

54:50.921 --> 54:52.641
[SPEAKER_00]: But normally we don't share that.

54:58.603 --> 55:04.945
[SPEAKER_00]: I think when you look at the sky, you say you see a sky, but what you really see is really light.

55:05.806 --> 55:07.646
[SPEAKER_00]: So some teachings say something like that.

55:08.627 --> 55:08.847
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

55:09.727 --> 55:10.888
[SPEAKER_00]: He said he'll tell you later.

55:10.908 --> 55:18.871
[SPEAKER_00]: Rinpoche, here's another question.

55:19.151 --> 55:24.893
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have any advice on how to apply this practice to strong emotions?

55:26.761 --> 55:33.503
[SPEAKER_00]: and sensations in the body, such as fear, same as thoughts or are they different?

55:34.104 --> 55:34.384
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

55:35.404 --> 55:39.826
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the strong emotion and this body sensation the same as a thought?

55:39.926 --> 55:42.307
[SPEAKER_00]: Should it be dealt with the same as a thought or is it different?

55:48.969 --> 55:51.370
[SPEAKER_00]: I think emotions

55:54.098 --> 55:56.901
[SPEAKER_00]: It's part of what we call nam tok or thought.

55:58.683 --> 55:59.924
[SPEAKER_00]: So we need a training.

56:00.685 --> 56:08.092
[SPEAKER_00]: I think gom, when you say gom, it means familiarization, I think, literally translation.

56:09.814 --> 56:16.380
[SPEAKER_00]: So the practice, I think we have to get quite familiar with it.

56:16.901 --> 56:18.082
[SPEAKER_00]: We have to do it again and again.

56:20.277 --> 56:38.090
[SPEAKER_00]: So the more you kind of get a little bit experience into resting in the state, nature of mind, then there will be less problem with all the, less problem which relate with your thoughts.

56:39.231 --> 56:40.552
[SPEAKER_00]: So emotion is also thoughts.

56:42.502 --> 56:58.147
[SPEAKER_00]: I have some friends who didn't do much practice and then suddenly they had some emotional problem and then comes to me and say how to meditate so then it's difficult when you're already like ups and downs so I think we have to

57:00.281 --> 57:04.944
[SPEAKER_00]: When everything is sort of okay, we have to practice, practice.

57:05.944 --> 57:13.689
[SPEAKER_00]: And once we really get quite familiar with this practice, then emotion will not bother so much.

57:13.709 --> 57:17.611
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite a good example is like riding a horse.

57:18.791 --> 57:20.873
[SPEAKER_00]: So if you're very good at riding a horse,

57:21.753 --> 57:24.055
[SPEAKER_00]: You become very violent riding a horse.

57:24.796 --> 57:28.198
[SPEAKER_00]: Then no matter how much the horse jumps, it cannot throw you out.

57:29.139 --> 57:37.926
[SPEAKER_00]: So that previous training, and not without much previous training, you just ride on a wild horse and then you will be thrown out.

57:38.867 --> 57:40.308
[SPEAKER_00]: It happened to me in Tibet once.

57:42.209 --> 57:42.890
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite painful.

57:44.386 --> 57:50.669
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think what we need is previously practice, practice, and then get familiar with that.

57:50.849 --> 58:01.894
[SPEAKER_00]: And then naturally, all the problems related with your mind will naturally be less and disappear.

58:05.475 --> 58:11.518
[SPEAKER_00]: This question is, if we let our thoughts and emotions just be,

58:13.101 --> 58:22.432
[SPEAKER_00]: then, and we just stay and watch, does that mean we will then do nothing in life and be very passive or numb?

58:25.156 --> 58:25.897
[SPEAKER_00]: Passive?

58:27.759 --> 58:28.260
[SPEAKER_00]: Vegetable.

58:30.202 --> 58:31.684
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it?

58:33.213 --> 58:37.017
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a friend who told me a story.

58:37.658 --> 58:44.405
[SPEAKER_00]: One guy went to see a doctor with a carrot in his nose and a banana in his ear.

58:45.827 --> 58:49.190
[SPEAKER_00]: And he went to see a doctor and said, Doctor, I have a problem.

58:49.210 --> 58:52.494
[SPEAKER_00]: And the doctor says, Yes, you have an eating disorder.

59:02.580 --> 59:04.962
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't think we are talking about something like a vegetable.

59:05.702 --> 59:18.131
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you talk about the meditation, there is a, I don't know how to describe in English, maybe Sean can do, jingwa, mukpa, thippa, gupa.

59:18.471 --> 59:19.172
[SPEAKER_00]: So there are many

59:20.554 --> 59:22.637
[SPEAKER_00]: many kind of where you can fall into.

59:23.298 --> 59:27.364
[SPEAKER_00]: So therefore you need a good instruction, step by step, we have to learn it.

59:28.025 --> 59:31.330
[SPEAKER_00]: So it could be sometimes jingwa, then maybe become vegetable.

59:31.650 --> 59:32.071
[SPEAKER_01]: Drowsiness.

59:32.151 --> 59:34.194
[SPEAKER_00]: Then mukpa, maybe you are sleeping.

59:36.617 --> 59:42.484
[SPEAKER_00]: So there are so many wrongs you have to go through.

59:42.564 --> 59:43.966
[SPEAKER_00]: Erroneous paths.

59:44.526 --> 59:49.712
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, so this needs to be some skill master and then like guidance.

59:54.263 --> 01:00:01.966
[SPEAKER_01]: So when one begins to practice meditation, there are a number of erroneous paths that one can fall into.

01:00:02.666 --> 01:00:08.748
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's essential to kind of clear these up before one begins a kind of serious life retreat or something.

01:00:09.168 --> 01:00:12.049
[SPEAKER_01]: As such, one should be able to recognize the dullness of mind.

01:00:12.569 --> 01:00:14.270
[SPEAKER_01]: Similarly, then, a kind of...

01:00:14.970 --> 01:00:17.612
[SPEAKER_01]: a blankness, kind of daydreamy state of mind.

01:00:17.692 --> 01:00:19.614
[SPEAKER_01]: Similarly then, lethargy, etc.

01:00:20.094 --> 01:00:32.984
[SPEAKER_01]: All of these should be recognized and as such, when you experience them, then you should apply the respective antidote so as to clear them, so as to be able to sustain or continue your meditation practice.

01:00:40.889 --> 01:00:50.132
[SPEAKER_00]: Rinpoche, how can peace-abiding, I assume they mean shamatha meditation or single-pointed meditation, help young school children?

01:00:51.152 --> 01:00:53.172
[SPEAKER_00]: And how can children practice meditation?

01:00:54.093 --> 01:00:54.493
[SPEAKER_00]: How can?

01:00:54.853 --> 01:00:56.333
[SPEAKER_00]: How can children practice?

01:00:58.934 --> 01:01:01.915
[SPEAKER_00]: What was the first one?

01:01:02.555 --> 01:01:07.717
[SPEAKER_00]: How can shamatha or single-pointed meditation help young school kids?

01:01:09.920 --> 01:01:12.841
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, of course, I think it's very helpful, I think.

01:01:19.902 --> 01:01:37.285
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how to explain properly, but Matthew Ricard, our Dharma brother, my Dharma brother, who is also a scientist, they had a lot of research done on how the children can meditate and use the one-pointedness or shamatha.

01:01:38.011 --> 01:01:42.493
[SPEAKER_00]: by using teddy bear, small teddy bear, you know, for very young children.

01:01:43.733 --> 01:01:55.798
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think they did some research and keeping the teddy bear on the chest, maybe they are lying down and keeping, and then the breathing in and out, the teddy bear goes up and down or something.

01:01:56.098 --> 01:01:58.419
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is a very much a good result.

01:01:59.619 --> 01:02:00.219
[SPEAKER_00]: Good result.

01:02:00.279 --> 01:02:07.482
[SPEAKER_00]: And even I think, once we visit one of the school in New York, I don't remember which one,

01:02:09.353 --> 01:02:09.674
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

01:02:10.354 --> 01:02:11.316
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, Dwight School.

01:02:12.117 --> 01:02:13.118
[SPEAKER_00]: Long time ago, I forgot.

01:02:13.698 --> 01:02:16.181
[SPEAKER_00]: So we talked a little bit to the children.

01:02:16.222 --> 01:02:18.304
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, in America, you don't teach religion.

01:02:18.925 --> 01:02:20.246
[SPEAKER_00]: But we just say, stay.

01:02:21.828 --> 01:02:22.449
[SPEAKER_00]: It's okay, no?

01:02:26.168 --> 01:02:29.652
[SPEAKER_00]: We just said like in the beginning, just stay five minutes or three minutes.

01:02:29.952 --> 01:02:33.816
[SPEAKER_00]: Just stay quietly without doing anything.

01:02:33.836 --> 01:02:36.478
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't really teach like meditation, but just sit quietly.

01:02:37.239 --> 01:02:39.521
[SPEAKER_00]: And they said it was really, really cool.

01:02:42.424 --> 01:02:43.645
[SPEAKER_00]: And they said really helpful.

01:02:44.886 --> 01:02:49.691
[SPEAKER_00]: So like that, I think many people, they used very, very helpful because they...

01:02:53.877 --> 01:02:54.577
[SPEAKER_00]: One has a

01:03:10.847 --> 01:03:24.771
[SPEAKER_01]: If one has an ethical kind of basis, thereupon the development of concentration is relatively straightforward, and there within, the development of wisdom within the person comes quite naturally.

01:03:27.361 --> 01:03:33.184
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure these days now many teachers, they talk about the science of meditation.

01:03:33.665 --> 01:03:36.246
[SPEAKER_00]: So I don't have, I'm sure you must have heard.

01:03:36.346 --> 01:03:42.810
[SPEAKER_00]: But one thing I can tell you is that because when we meditate or visualization or meditation,

01:03:43.268 --> 01:04:12.710
[SPEAKER_00]: you can't really see how we are progressing because we are so used to seeing some result like a stock market up and down so we don't really see but when they test in the lab they can really see that even one week of some one-pointed meditation you really have big change in your mind so since we can't really tangible tangible then we don't see so I have a story we have a retreat center in Nepal

01:04:13.591 --> 01:04:20.919
[SPEAKER_00]: And we sent two of the monks who had just finished the retreat to a lab in medicine for the science research.

01:04:22.161 --> 01:04:29.669
[SPEAKER_00]: And then we sent a third monk who was the administrator of the monastery in a place called Bihar in Budhgaya.

01:04:29.689 --> 01:04:31.351
[SPEAKER_00]: There was a big mess there.

01:04:32.195 --> 01:04:40.099
[SPEAKER_00]: So these three monks went to the lab, but we forgot to mention the two was the retreatant and one is not retreatant.

01:04:40.779 --> 01:04:44.741
[SPEAKER_00]: But we received an email from the scientists saying, what's wrong with the third one?

01:04:47.522 --> 01:04:57.807
[SPEAKER_00]: So it really, of course, three year definite changes, but even one week we tried meditation, tremendous changes, progress.

01:04:58.547 --> 01:05:00.608
[SPEAKER_00]: But because we can't really feel or see it,

01:05:01.496 --> 01:05:08.260
[SPEAKER_00]: And weight, you can go on a scale, you can see how much pound you lose, but here you cannot see.

01:05:09.181 --> 01:05:10.802
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's definitely big changes.

01:05:11.002 --> 01:05:15.865
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're really interested, maybe go in the lab and you will find the results.

01:05:15.885 --> 01:05:21.949
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think this one point in his mind, really, little bit meditation makes big changes in your mind.

01:05:23.970 --> 01:05:29.253
[SPEAKER_00]: That leads to a question maybe a lot of people are asking now, which is that mindfulness meditation,

01:05:29.961 --> 01:05:39.149
[SPEAKER_00]: mindfulness meditation has become quite popular in the West as a standalone, not necessarily affiliated with a complete course of Buddhist study.

01:05:39.869 --> 01:05:41.010
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you think that's a good thing?

01:05:41.170 --> 01:05:46.755
[SPEAKER_00]: It's being practiced in corporations, in sports teams, fitness centers, yoga studios?

01:05:49.958 --> 01:05:53.060
[SPEAKER_00]: First of all, anything that helps is a good thing, I think.

01:05:54.241 --> 01:05:58.425
[SPEAKER_00]: And also with... How's that?

01:05:59.590 --> 01:05:59.870
[SPEAKER_00]: OK.

01:06:05.835 --> 01:06:08.337
[SPEAKER_00]: But is it Buddhism then?

01:06:08.377 --> 01:06:09.257
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

01:06:10.618 --> 01:06:13.601
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, I think, because ...

01:06:17.568 --> 01:06:27.755
[SPEAKER_00]: It sort of opens the door to the people to get the taste of meditation.

01:06:29.576 --> 01:06:32.258
[SPEAKER_00]: So people maybe will just do that.

01:06:33.138 --> 01:06:36.020
[SPEAKER_00]: Also very useful, helpful, that's good.

01:06:36.601 --> 01:06:39.763
[SPEAKER_00]: But then maybe through that, people want to go deeper into it.

01:06:40.403 --> 01:06:45.845
[SPEAKER_00]: then we must go into the traditional way of practicing and all that.

01:06:46.225 --> 01:07:06.272
[SPEAKER_00]: So yes, I think it is very good that it gives people an opportunity who maybe don't feel ready to go into deeper practice, but still benefit from the meditation, the technique or method from the Buddhist tradition.

01:07:09.157 --> 01:07:12.159
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps we should close with one final question or how's our time?

01:07:14.080 --> 01:07:14.561
[SPEAKER_00]: We have time?

01:07:15.822 --> 01:07:17.082
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it okay to do a few more?

01:07:17.443 --> 01:07:17.623
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

01:07:18.023 --> 01:07:18.283
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

01:07:18.884 --> 01:07:20.545
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a very profound question.

01:07:21.665 --> 01:07:21.946
[SPEAKER_00]: Deep.

01:07:22.446 --> 01:07:22.986
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

01:07:25.308 --> 01:07:28.170
[SPEAKER_00]: What advice do you have for people who get sleepy when they're sitting?

01:07:29.931 --> 01:07:30.271
[SPEAKER_00]: Coffee.

01:07:45.569 --> 01:07:47.150
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe I can share this with you.

01:07:47.270 --> 01:07:50.251
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a story of my friend Matthew.

01:07:51.732 --> 01:08:03.157
[SPEAKER_00]: When he was a young scientist and very interested in Dharma, very enthusiast, and he came to Darjeeling to meet his first teacher, Kanjur Rinpoche.

01:08:04.182 --> 01:08:09.866
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was sitting there, and then he saw the monks doing prayer and falling asleep.

01:08:10.826 --> 01:08:12.487
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was so amazed.

01:08:12.507 --> 01:08:15.229
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, how can they sleep during the prayer?

01:08:16.069 --> 01:08:19.391
[SPEAKER_00]: But later on, he's the number one sleeper in the puja.

01:08:22.959 --> 01:08:39.802
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, I think most of the Buddhist practice, you can always, a way to wake yourself, you can get up and walk, walk a little bit here and there, and then sit and meditate.

01:08:42.023 --> 01:08:48.944
[SPEAKER_00]: You can use any kind of method so that you don't feel completely like dullness.

01:08:49.705 --> 01:08:51.425
[SPEAKER_00]: Even in Vinaya for the monks,

01:08:52.722 --> 01:09:05.739
[SPEAKER_00]: When they are meditating, then it describes that they should make a small ball and make a string, and then you throw the ball and it doesn't really hit, but it comes and then you wake.

01:09:06.660 --> 01:09:11.366
[SPEAKER_00]: So in the Vinaya, Buddha described that for the monks.

01:09:12.258 --> 01:09:14.320
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'm sure you can use any method.

01:09:14.420 --> 01:09:21.685
[SPEAKER_00]: And maybe in some sacred tradition practice, you're not supposed to get up, but that according to that particular sadhana.

01:09:22.286 --> 01:09:25.508
[SPEAKER_00]: But otherwise, I think whatever it makes you feel awake.

01:09:25.528 --> 01:09:29.731
[SPEAKER_00]: And also meditation, you don't feel comfortable on the floor.

01:09:29.771 --> 01:09:30.872
[SPEAKER_00]: You can sit on the chair.

01:09:33.654 --> 01:09:38.218
[SPEAKER_00]: So whatever is helpful to the mind to awaken oneself.

01:09:40.625 --> 01:09:43.488
[SPEAKER_00]: In Tibet, sometimes the yogis have long hair.

01:09:44.089 --> 01:09:47.613
[SPEAKER_00]: They tie the hair up in the ceiling.

01:09:51.678 --> 01:09:53.881
[SPEAKER_00]: So if someone has a ponytail, you can use that.

01:09:55.382 --> 01:09:57.024
[SPEAKER_00]: That's harder for a monk, Rinpoche.

01:09:57.044 --> 01:09:59.207
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be difficult for a monk.

01:10:01.159 --> 01:10:11.590
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think a practitioner, I think it's very important to have a kind of good, especially the beginner, I think, to have a uplifting space or atmosphere.

01:10:14.092 --> 01:10:16.094
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember once H.F.G.

01:10:16.174 --> 01:10:24.303
[SPEAKER_00]: Khyentse Rinpoche was asked by a Bhutanese scholar, there is a place in Bhutan, and people said the place belongs to Longchenpa.

01:10:25.898 --> 01:10:28.000
[SPEAKER_00]: And do you think that place belongs to Longchenpa?

01:10:28.020 --> 01:10:30.823
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's supposed to be eight places of Longchenpa in Bhutan.

01:10:31.443 --> 01:10:35.327
[SPEAKER_00]: And the first question Khyentse Rinpoche asked her is that, does it have a good view?

01:10:36.889 --> 01:10:38.070
[SPEAKER_00]: Does this place have a good view?

01:10:39.411 --> 01:10:42.034
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, no, it can't be Longchenpa's place.

01:10:43.566 --> 01:10:51.768
[SPEAKER_00]: So even great master like Longchenpa, if you look at the places he chooses to practice, it's always like high up mountain and very spacious.

01:10:53.028 --> 01:11:03.471
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think that's also maybe he's not falling asleep, but it helps enhance the practice of Dzogchen or nature of mind.

01:11:06.911 --> 01:11:07.692
[SPEAKER_00]: We have a few more.

01:11:11.229 --> 01:11:19.112
[SPEAKER_00]: Rinpoche, isn't uncontrived also a concept when you say practice uncontrived?

01:11:19.492 --> 01:11:20.932
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that also not a concept?

01:11:21.592 --> 01:11:26.634
[SPEAKER_01]: I think the

01:11:40.435 --> 01:11:51.199
[SPEAKER_01]: In the text, when it says one should rest uncontrived, free, and at ease in that state, it's not thinking about doing something in an uncontrived state.

01:11:51.239 --> 01:11:53.340
[SPEAKER_01]: This is simply a way of resting.

01:11:53.740 --> 01:11:59.282
[SPEAKER_01]: One just drops everything so there's no contrivance and remains within that state.

01:11:59.843 --> 01:12:04.424
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a question of thinking that one is practicing in an uncontrived way.

01:12:11.141 --> 01:12:11.882
[SPEAKER_00]: .

01:12:13.082 --> 01:12:23.587
[SPEAKER_01]: So maybe in Tibetan, when you say or in English, it's translated here as uncontrived, maybe there's a slight kind of difference.

01:12:23.607 --> 01:12:36.413
[SPEAKER_01]: In the Tibetan, it simply means to drop everything and simply kind of remain without any kind of, I'll have to use the English, of course, but without any contrivance, to simply to be without that, resting in the natural state.

01:12:43.877 --> 01:12:47.278
[SPEAKER_00]: But this one is a good question, but there's one word that is unintelligible.

01:12:47.298 --> 01:12:50.199
[SPEAKER_00]: So if the person who wrote it could just maybe shout out this word.

